## Context and authority

Filed from replicator at the author's request, 2026-11-27, after an Imagine
session with the author (step 1.5 of the author's Toulouse Games timeline:
clarify the interface between assessment with and without code execution).
Every decision below is the author's. Ruled: implement **now, without
aliases** (the author chose this over aliases-until-the-Games). In-flight
tickets 1572-1588 keep their text; the mapping below translates them.
Supersedes IDH1580 remark A4's "stop and offer before deep search" (see
Decision 4). Data contracts are unchanged.

## 1. Problem

The four verbs (`assess`, `reproduce`, `probe`, `report`) blur two things:
the author could not tell that "assessment" has a no-run and a run level, and
`report` is an output, not a stage. `assess` and `probe` are too generic. The
identification review has no verb, and the robustness plan starts from a
generic menu rather than from identified threats.

## 2. Situation

Evidence in replicator: the 0003 pilot (`replications/chain-pilot-0003/`),
the Gavard run (`replications/gavard2026climate/`), and the author's 24
report remarks (IDH1580). The Games are on 2026-12-08; the skill will run on
Brodeur's candidate papers in the coming days, so one skill version must
serve the whole batch.

## 3. State of the art

I4R General template (Zenodo 23226406): sections Introduction, Computational
Reproducibility, Discrepancies with preregistration, Robustness
Reproduction/Replication, Conclusion; I4R definitions of computational,
recreate, robustness, direct and conceptual reproducibility. The
identification review is our addition (references/identification-review.md).

## 4. Alternatives considered

1. One verb per report section. 2. Verbs stay workflow stops and each writes
its section. 3. Status quo with renamed execution depths. The author chose
1 AND 2: verbs are cumulative stages, each adding its section.

## 5. Decisions (author)

1. **Cumulative stages.** Asking for stage k runs stages 1..k. Nothing is
   skipped: every stage ends with a coherence pass over sections 1..k, which
   revises an earlier section that a new finding contradicts and records the
   revision. Valid computations are not recomputed (idempotence), but every
   section is reread.
2. **The report is the output of every stage**, compiled each time, cut at
   the last written section; the I4R table shows "not yet run" where nothing
   ran. One Zotero note, updated at each stage, replaces the two note types.
3. **Report order follows the science**, not the I4R template: identification
   comes right after the introduction (we add the section, so we place it).
4. **Names are per paper, descriptive:**

| # | Verb | Section | Runs code | Stop |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `inventory` | Claim and package: main result chosen from the paper, package inventory, the four provision rows | no | none |
| 2 | `identify` | Identification: causal graph, estimation faithful to the graph, credibility of the graph against other studies and common sense; strong model plus targeted verified checks | no | none: runs unattended by default. Deep literature search is out of the chain; it is a separate request when the author writes a comment or publishes. The section ends with a list "graph points to support by a literature review before publication" |
| 3 | `rerun` | Reproduction: rerun or transcribe, compare every printed statistic, the two reproduction rows | yes | scope choice at the start, before any computation (below) |
| 4 | `challenge` | Robustness: plan derived from stage 2 threats, including at least one check outside the paper's statistical frame; signed checks run | yes | plan signature, no timeout |
| 5 | `complete` | Conclusion, introduction written last, AI Disclosure, final coherence pass | no | sending is an outward act, always the author's |

5. **`rerun` scope stop.** Three options in plain words: *main result* (the
   table or figure chosen at `inventory`), *I4R scope* (what I4R asks teams
   to reproduce at the Games), *whole paper* (all tables and figures,
   appendices and SI). If the caller specified the scope, no question. Else
   ask the user; with no answer in 5 minutes, continue with the minimal
   option (main result). Purpose: the author validates scope before long runs.
6. **Mapping for in-flight tickets and docs:** `assess` -> `inventory` (and
   the identification part -> `identify`); `reproduce` -> `rerun`; `probe` ->
   `challenge`; `report` -> written by every stage, assembled by `complete`.
   Frozen replication folders keep the old names (they record the version
   that produced them).
   The mapping has a limited lifetime (author ruling): it lives only while
   tickets written with the old names are open, then it is deleted from the
   skill and docs; git history keeps it.

## 6. Implementation notes and open items

- "I4R scope" must be defined from I4R sources (Brodeur's pre-games slides,
  the templates), not from replicator's one-report observation that I4R
  scored rows on Tables 1-3.
- The 5-minute question needs a timed-question mechanism (wake arm or
  equivalent) with a default, specified per runtime (Claude Code, Codex, Pi).
- Unchanged: assessment.json, criteres, cra_*/crr_*, out/*.csv contracts,
  blind mode, scope (whole paper default vs selected target), signing rule.
- After landing: one rehearsal run on a paper outside replicator's
  `data/splits/dev-exposed-2026-11-26.csv`, then freeze a skill version for
  the Brodeur batch, cited in each report's AI Disclosure.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Receiving Imagine records facts, overlap with 1572-1588 (notably 1580),
  ownership and standing rulings.
- [ ] SKILL.md and references implement Decisions 1-6, without aliases.
- [ ] One rehearsal run produces a compiled report at each stage; its
  version is recorded for the Brodeur batch.
